WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.199
Business plan highlights
Strategy

00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:06.230
Bill McCarthy: Thank you, Chairman.

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:11.589
The business plan to deliver all the things
we would do as an organisation are covered

00:00:11.589 --> 00:00:18.620
in paragraphs 18 to 28 in the narrative and
also, they are summarised in the delivery

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.860
status tables in the annex.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.160
I think that is self explanatory.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:30.340
I do not intend to go into that in much more
detail.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.559
I would pick out just two things, I think.

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:39.579
First of all a reference to David's Chief
Executive's report, where he is talking about

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:44.199
the strategy work that we launched right at
the start -- if you remember, it was about

00:00:44.199 --> 00:00:50.359
the time of the last Board meeting -- and
that has been carrying on a lot of energy

00:00:50.359 --> 00:00:55.750
since then, with yesterday's AGM being a real
staging post.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.829
Choice and competition
The other thing I would pick out is in the

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:06.370
status report and it is the discussions we
are having with Monitor around choice and

00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:14.960
competition and how best they can be applied
in healthcare to improve outcomes for patients,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.350
including better experience.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:25.080
That has taken a bit longer than we had hoped
-- we had hoped between us to be able to put

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:33.520
out some guidance early in the summer -- and
I think that probably reflects that it is

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.869
one of the areas where there is a paucity
of evidence.

00:01:36.869 --> 00:01:45.829
We are committed to be a system that does
work on evidence and in this area, even taking

00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:50.980
from the international experience, the direct
evidence of where best competition and choice

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.429
can work to improve outcomes is fairly limited.

00:01:53.429 --> 00:02:02.249
The approach we are now adopting with Monitor
is actively to seek out, collect and publish

00:02:02.249 --> 00:02:07.049
that evidence as we go along, so we can be
a learning organisation.

00:02:07.049 --> 00:02:13.280
As we discover more about where these forces
really work for patients and just as importantly,

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.129
where they do not, we will be capturing that
and making it public, so that we can give

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:24.680
the best possible guidance to commissioners
and indeed, to providers, in the NHS.

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:27.070
Malcolm Grant: This is so important to get
away from ideological preconceptions and to

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:30.790
get to a much more pragmatic focus around
the needs of patients.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:32.310
Good, thank you for that.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:33.819
May we turn to Jo?

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:34.819
Organisational health
Clarification

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:36.849
Jo Anne Wass: Yes, thank you, Chairman.

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:42.170
May I take us briefly to section D of the
pack of material?

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:47.040
This section is headed 'Organisational Health'
and I think I just ought to point out -- I

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.819
know the Board appreciates this, but for the
people in the room and on the live stream

00:02:50.819 --> 00:02:53.810
-- that the organisation we are talking about
is NHS England.

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:57.459
Whilst the majority of the pack looks outwards
and looks at the performance of the whole

00:02:57.459 --> 00:03:01.239
NHS, this bit of the pack is about the performance
of NHS.

00:03:01.239 --> 00:03:05.360
It is important, I think, because I talked
to somebody at the AGM last night who thought

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:10.750
we employed the 1.3million staff in the NHS;
and the workforce bits I am going to talk

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.400
about are about the 6,000 staff that NHS England
employs.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.880
Staff barometer background
The bit I would like to focus on, given the

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:24.810
time constraints, is the results of the first
staff barometer.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:28.670
What you have here are the high level results;
the detailed results will be looked at in

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:29.989
the Remuneration Committee.

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:37.420
As Malcolm said, in Committee is where we
do most of the work of the Board.

00:03:37.420 --> 00:03:41.610
In previous meetings, we have talked about
the difficult transition process which staff

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:42.689
have just been through.

00:03:42.689 --> 00:03:47.519
We have talked about that quite a lot and
we decided we wanted to take the temperature

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:52.150
of the organisation quite early on to get
a sense of how people were feeling.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.549
We have been working on this with Michael
West, who is an academic from Lancaster University

00:03:57.549 --> 00:04:02.780
who has worked over the last ten years on
the bigger NHS survey and has done some very

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:08.219
interesting work linking staff experience
and patient experience in the NHS.

00:04:08.219 --> 00:04:13.050
What his research proves is that there is
a very direct correlation between staff experience

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:17.940
and patient experience and in fact, the experience
of staff in organisations is one of the strongest

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:20.209
determinants of patient mortality in organisations.

00:04:20.209 --> 00:04:22.180
It is very interesting stuff.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.610
Staff barometer results
What we have done here is that, rather than

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:29.340
ask people to do the whole survey, we extracted
questions from the staff survey which we thought

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:34.280
were relevant to an organisation that was
in a start up mode, which has allowed us to

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:40.710
do comparisons against the wider NHS workforce
survey results.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:47.560
In the little graphs on page eight you can
see our results benchmarked against the wider

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:52.570
staff survey results.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:58.390
As well as the questions, we also gave people
the chance to give us free text comments and

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:06.819
we got quite a lot of those; and you will
see at the bottom of page seven that we have

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:09.240
correlated those, had a look at them and put
them into three categories.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:13.220
We think there are three sorts of things which
people were telling us about.

00:05:13.220 --> 00:05:17.470
One was getting the basics right: sorting
out IT, sorting out accommodation and sorting

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:19.960
out basic policies and processes.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:23.900
The second one was about clarity of roles
and objectives, which I guess is probably

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.569
not unusual in a new organisation but still,
you can see from the quantity of data that

00:05:28.569 --> 00:05:31.199
that is an area where we need to do some work.

00:05:31.199 --> 00:05:34.470
The third one was about tackling bureaucracy
and hierarchy.

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:40.139
It is the first time we have ever had a national
organisation like this and some of our processes

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:41.650
are, I think, a bit clunky at the moment.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:44.280
It is about that, really.

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.260
I do want to say, 'Thank you very much' to
everyone who filled it in.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:50.580
You will see the response rate was not marvellous
and one of the things we need to do is get

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.729
the response rate up.

00:05:52.729 --> 00:05:54.289
It was about 36% of the workforce.

00:05:54.289 --> 00:05:58.020
Next steps
The important thing, of course, is what we

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.259
do now with this.

00:05:59.259 --> 00:06:03.330
One of the ways in which we will get the response
rate up is showing people that we are listening

00:06:03.330 --> 00:06:05.380
and that we are doing something about it.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:13.509
What has therefore happened since we did the
barometer is that each national directorate,

00:06:13.509 --> 00:06:20.360
each region and each area team has been given
their own results; and we have each Director

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:24.919
responsible for those units within the organisation
to sit down with the staff, share the results

00:06:24.919 --> 00:06:28.949
and do their own action plan and to put those
action plans on the intranet.

00:06:28.949 --> 00:06:33.710
They are not all on there yet, but by the
time we have finished there will be an action

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:37.110
plan on the intranet for each region, each
area team and each national directorate.

00:06:37.110 --> 00:06:42.729
There is also a national action plan on there,
which explains the programme of work that

00:06:42.729 --> 00:06:45.319
we are going to oversee at national level.

00:06:45.319 --> 00:06:50.060
We have set up a group, which David chairs,
that is looking at seven priority areas; and

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.379
that is based on what people told us in the
staff barometer and a meeting that we had

00:06:54.379 --> 00:06:59.050
to review the OD strategy, which Victor, Bill
and David were involved in.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:04.600
The seven areas -- very briefly -- are accelerating
the PDR process, which is really important

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:10.230
for getting clarity of roles and objectives
for people; improving internal communications;

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:15.480
agile working, by which we mean making sure
people have the right accommodation and the

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:21.940
IT kit to be able to do their jobs; leadership;
making team working more effective across

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:25.660
the organisation -- we have previously called
that 'matrix working' but we are starting

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:31.520
to call it 'team working' now; health and
wellbeing of staff; and establishing a network

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:36.440
of workplace champions/excellent organisation
champions.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:41.490
At least one person in every building in which
we have staff will be a workplace champion

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:45.960
that we can have a two way dialogue with and
can provide us with real time feedback about

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.229
what we are doing.

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:53.500
Each of those seven work streams we are going
to ask an area director to lead, because most

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:58.419
of the staff are in area teams and because
area directors are very experienced -- a lot

00:07:58.419 --> 00:08:02.080
of them have been chief executives and have
done this in their own organisations -- and

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.790
each one will be support by a national director.

00:08:05.790 --> 00:08:09.680
We had the first meeting of that group earlier
this week and the next one is in about a month's

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:10.680
time.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.509
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:16.139
Q&amp;A
Margaret Casely Hayford: It is a really great

00:08:16.139 --> 00:08:18.610
piece of analysis and really helpful, thank
you.

00:08:18.610 --> 00:08:23.840
I had just one question and that was about
staff engagement and how you were measuring

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:33.390
that, because there appears to be a good level
of staff engagement from the graph here, but

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:39.350
the numbers of people who have actually participated
must demonstrate a level of engagement and

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:40.980
that slightly conflicts with this.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:45.800
Jo Anne Wass: Yes, I think that is right.

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:47.130
There is a series of questions.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:52.450
I think there are about 20 questions and the
analysts have split them into those headings.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:58.650
The questions that fed the staff engagement
graph were things like, 'I have opportunities

00:08:58.650 --> 00:09:01.950
to show initiative', '...to make suggestions',
'...to make improvements', etc.

00:09:01.950 --> 00:09:04.630
You are absolutely right, though.

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:08.360
The executive team have had a conversation
about the fact that for the next one, which

00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:12.720
is later in the Autumn, we really need to
get the response rate up, because the validity

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:15.370
of the results is only as good as the response
rates.

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:16.920
Malcolm Grant: Okay, thank you.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:18.170
Financial highlights
Paul Baumann

00:09:18.170 --> 00:09:21.390
Chief Financial Officer
I will briefly, then, pick out the financial

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.390
highlights.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:25.320
You will see in the main text that I have
pulled out a few of them, but I would refer

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:32.420
you to section C and to the summary at page
15 in section C which, to me, picks out the

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.850
financial highlights in the clearest fashion.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:39.560
I am going to distinguish, really, between
CCG performance and the performance of the

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.010
direct commissioners, briefly.

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:46.270
There are no variances of significance, as
you can see, in the CCGs overall.

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:51.680
And indeed the risk analysis, which each individual
CCG now does and feeds to us, suggests that

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:58.180
any risks they have got, and they are particularly
around activity, particularly acute activity,

00:09:58.180 --> 00:10:02.610
and quick delivery, can actually be covered
off by reserves and contingencies which they

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:05.800
have quite rightly put into the plans as they
constructed them.

00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:08.380
Notes of caution with CCG performance
There are just two notes of caution I would

00:10:08.380 --> 00:10:11.940
sound about CCG performance as it is summarised
here.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:16.840
One of which is the very differential resilience
of individual CCGs, which obviously gets masked

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:19.720
in the totality of this.

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:23.251
You will be aware that we have nine CCGs who
agreed a deficit plan with us.

00:10:23.251 --> 00:10:28.680
That is because there just was not a credible
way they were going to be able to live within

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:29.680
their resources.

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:33.910
There are another 14 CCGs who, in the risk
analysis that they have submitted, have indicated

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:38.750
that if all the risks that they can see around
them materialise, they do not yet see enough

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:41.920
mitigation to get themselves back up across
the line.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:44.770
So we have got quite a mixed bag of CCG strength.

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:48.420
That should be no surprise to anyone from
the discussions we have had already.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:51.780
Quality of QIPP delivery
And the second thing is the quality of QIPP

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:52.780
delivery.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:56.290
I had intended to put the QIPP slide into
this pack, and it will be in future ones,

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:59.110
but it does not appear to have found its way
into it.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:05.190
QIPP delivery, the delivery of savings from
productivity and other types of improvement,

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:06.550
is lagging a bit behind plan.

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:11.670
It is at about 86% of plan, which historically
is not a bad percentage, but we don't aim

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:14.650
for 86%, for obvious reasons.

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:19.440
Whilst the CCGs are forecasting that they
will get that back up to about 94% by the

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:22.900
end of the year, that is obviously quite a
cause for concern.

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:27.690
And if you dig a little bit deeper, the savings
they are finding most difficult to deliver,

00:11:27.690 --> 00:11:33.250
which we are now distinguishing carefully
from other sorts of savings, are the transformational

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:34.250
savings.

00:11:34.250 --> 00:11:37.460
I guess, again, there is no great surprise
in those, but there is a performance which

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:43.080
is pretty strong on: the transactional stuff,
the routine stuff, the day-in-day-out stuff

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:46.920
and rather weaker on the transformational
stuff, which brings us a bit back to where

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:52.000
David was in his introduction, which is that
we have really got to switch the dial up on

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:56.080
the strategic change we are trying to do,
because it is becoming apparent that it is

00:11:56.080 --> 00:11:58.320
the bit that is holding us back, and will
continue to hold us back.

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:00.510
So, that is really the CCGs.

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:04.470
Pressures with direct commissioning
Direct commissioning is, and we said this

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:09.250
when we talked about the plans earlier in
the year, the area where the pressures were

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:15.800
always going to be more difficult to meet
than they are in the CCG sector.

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:19.430
And it is true to say, if you just look at
the trends there, that the combination of

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:27.370
baseline issues that we have had in resolving
the shift of business from the PCT baselines

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:32.720
into specialised commissioning, and in particular
the limited visibility of activity, are giving

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:39.000
our specialised commissioning teams a very
big job of work to do to get a grip on their

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.950
numbers, and then to deliver within the budgets
that they have.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:46.580
So, in the numbers you have got here, four
of the ten area teams are showing adverse

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:48.710
variances, which are through here.

00:12:48.710 --> 00:12:53.660
The other six are flagging up, in a similar
fashion, risks around that, which, to a large

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:59.520
extent, they can mitigate once off, through
some combination of reserves and other measures.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:05.710
But we have quite a serious worry about the
ability to get all of that sorted out.

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:11.470
It is fair to say we are prioritising a lot
of action on the activity control side of

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:15.620
things at the moment; Barbara is personally
leading a big piece of work to try and, in

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:19.490
the next few weeks, really get the visibility
of that up and running.

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:22.890
But there is quite a bit of risk around there.

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:27.560
And, as you would expect, that is also reflected
in QIPP performance, which is weaker on the

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:32.550
part of direct commissioners, to date at about
70%, than it was in the CCGs.

00:13:32.550 --> 00:13:35.200
So we have got some work to do on QIPP as
well.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:36.590
Running costs and programme costs for NHS
England

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:41.260
So, the final thing to note is that the running
costs and programme costs of NHS England which

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:46.680
are, in large measure, the other line that
you can see in there, are running very positively,

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:47.680
year to date.

00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:51.191
I do not take any particular message out of
that; I think it is purely the phasing with

00:13:51.191 --> 00:13:56.640
which we take on our various activities in
the year, so we will see how that progresses

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:58.600
during the course of the year.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:13:59.970
Summary
So, in summary, there are significant challenges

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:05.420
we are addressing, and particular challenges
for the teams managing specialised commissioning.

00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:10.790
We are going to be carrying out a very in-depth
stock take around the month six numbers which

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:18.690
we work on in October, to try and get an even
stronger handle on where the year is headed

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:22.670
to, and potentially also trying to link through
to the longer term work, in terms of where

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:24.460
the underlying trends are going.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:28.630
And, obviously, we will bring that back to
the board in November, when we have the next

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:29.630
financial update.

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:32.280
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much.

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.350
Right, I think that concludes that item.

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:35.670
Thank you very much Paul.

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:40.650
May we turn now to the final item on the agenda,
which is the NHS England and CCG financial

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:41.730
plans for 13/14?

00:14:41.730 --> 00:14:46.080
Bill McCarthy: Do you want a couple of words
on the Board Assurance Framework?

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:53.440
Malcolm Grant: We will just have a brief review
of the Board Assurance Framework.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:57.980
Board Assurance Framework
Bill McCarthy

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:05.270
National Director: Policy
An update on the Board Assurance Framework

00:15:05.270 --> 00:15:11.890
I can confirm, in process terms, the executive
risk management group is meeting monthly under

00:15:11.890 --> 00:15:12.890
Barbara's chairmanship.

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:17.340
Barbara may want to say a word at the end
of this.

00:15:17.340 --> 00:15:24.320
And it is managing the strategic risks as
well as hearing about any other risks that

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:27.250
are brought to our attention from the organisation
itself.

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:30.020
New strategic risks: process immaturity
There are just two things I would highlight

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:34.930
from the report: two new strategic risks since
we last met.

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:41.750
Both of them are really about the newness
of the system.

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:47.080
One we have called the 'Immaturity of some
of the processes,' and I think we have been

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:50.200
hearing about some of that this morning.

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:57.670
So, when Jo was talking about getting our
staff fully engaged since we brought all of

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:02.620
the new staff on, that is part of developing
the processes that Paul was talking about:

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:03.750
the activity processes.

00:16:03.750 --> 00:16:10.870
So we are keeping a close eye on the development
of the processes, which will help us deliver

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:12.220
the right sort of outcomes for patients.

00:16:12.220 --> 00:16:13.280
New strategic risks: partnerships with other
parts of the system

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:18.600
And the second one is reflecting the discussion
here at the board last time, and is about

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:21.330
our partnerships with other parts of the system.

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:27.720
I know there was a concern that there are
more players, and, therefore, the potential

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:32.440
for a more fragmented way of supporting better
outcomes for patients.

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:37.090
We are putting a lot of time into developing
those relationships and, in that context,

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:45.350
I think having all of our partner organisations
nationally signing up to the strategic call

00:16:45.350 --> 00:16:51.860
to action, and participating very actively,
is a really important mitigation in aligning

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:53.810
our goals and our priorities.

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:59.500
So, we have put those on the register, because
they are important, but we are actively managing

00:16:59.500 --> 00:17:01.440
them both.

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:05.600
Barbara Hakin: As chairman of the risk management
committee I would just like to say I feel

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:10.170
I can give the board increasing assurance
that we have got in place all the systems

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:14.040
and processes for identifying, assessing and
managing risk.

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:18.110
Although I do not think we should underestimate
the complexities such a large organisation,

00:17:18.110 --> 00:17:25.480
geographically dispersed, and the need to
marry the bottom-up identification of risk

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:30.640
with that strategic consideration at national
level.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:34.240
We have had a couple of examples where I think
this has really worked brilliantly.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:38.320
Paul alluded to the activity work, but it
was actually the risk management systems that

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:44.390
brought to our attention, a few weeks ago,
the risks we were running because we did not

00:17:44.390 --> 00:17:48.480
have the precise activity information and
the visibility that we had been used to in

00:17:48.480 --> 00:17:49.480
the old system.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:54.610
We were able to escalate that to the executive
team, who were then able to put in place an

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:59.850
urgent external assessment of what needed
to be done to get us back on track.

00:17:59.850 --> 00:18:05.190
That work has now happened and we have got
a new project within NHS England to deliver

00:18:05.190 --> 00:18:06.190
better visibility.

00:18:06.190 --> 00:18:09.390
That is just a practical example of a way
it is really working.

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:15.760
I think we have a lot more to do on risk management
in terms of, 'Are we assessing and scoring

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:20.270
risks equally?' and the way that different
individuals do that.

00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:24.230
And I think we have more to do to work out
how we present, both to the board and the

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:30.720
audit committee, what you see, so that you
are able to see the biggest risks, and be

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:36.440
in a position to either identify those risks
or choose, as a board or audit committee,

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:38.470
further mitigating actions that you think
should be taken.

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:41.470
It is a work in progress, but I think we really
are beginning to get a good system in place.

00:18:41.470 --> 00:18:49.130
Ciarán Devane: It might just be worth dropping
in, as part of the framework, that the quality

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:56.540
and technical risk committee met for the first
time earlier this week, with Cyril Chantler

00:18:56.540 --> 00:18:59.890
as the expert independent chair.

00:18:59.890 --> 00:19:06.690
And what we are thinking of doing is three
things: firstly, starting off by saying, 'Are

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:12.450
the processes for identifying clinical risk
as they stand at the moment robust uniformly

00:19:12.450 --> 00:19:19.450
across NHS England?; secondly, working on
what continuous improvement of the outcomes

00:19:19.450 --> 00:19:25.670
would be; thirdly, trying to project forward
and say what risks will come down the track

00:19:25.670 --> 00:19:28.640
at us, because of an ageing population, amongst
other things: in a phrase, to take it in that

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:32.860
sequence, and get our own house in order first.

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:43.810
I think that will help with 
the framework.

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:51.300
Malcolm Grant: Okay, thank you very much.

00:19:51.300 --> 00:19:54.450
Paul Baumann
Chief Financial Officer

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:55.980
I can keep this very brief, Malcolm.

00:19:55.980 --> 00:20:01.470
This is just the formal culmination of the
process of plan-making at CCG level and area

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:02.470
team level.

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:06.140
It has been through rigorous scrutiny by the
regional teams, and indeed by the central

00:20:06.140 --> 00:20:07.140
teams.

00:20:07.140 --> 00:20:11.850
The Ops executive has worked hard on this,
and, finally, the finance and investment committee

00:20:11.850 --> 00:20:13.990
has spent a lot of its time looking at this
in-depth.

00:20:13.990 --> 00:20:18.770
So, the plans which are in front of you, and
the comments on them which are in the narrative,

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:25.420
are there simply for you to note and formally
approve, if you would, as a matter of record,

00:20:25.420 --> 00:20:26.510
at this stage of the year.

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:29.950
Malcolm Grant: May I invite the board to formally
approve the plan?

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:30.950
Thank you very much.

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:34.530
That brings us to the end of business for
this meeting.

00:20:34.530 --> 00:20:39.660
The only other business I have is to report
that the next meeting of the board will be

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:46.110
on the 8th November at Rivergate House, Newbury
Business Park in Berkshire.

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:51.200
I now propose that I shall close the formal
public meeting of the board, and the board

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:55.360
will then, shortly, move into private session,
as I indicated at the outset.

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:59.680
May I, on behalf of the whole board, thank
all those members of the public who have joined

00:20:59.680 --> 00:21:04.550
us this morning, and have been able to observe
the board in action?

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:05.790
And I now declare this meeting closed.

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:06.250
Thank you.

